---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';
import { availableServiceCount } from '../../data/services';

const contents = [
  { id: 'handful', label: 'A handful of emulators and a shell script' },
  { id: 'compare', label: 'Separate emulators or one runtime' },
  { id: 'opt-in', label: 'Heavy services wait until you ask' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'Start it' },
];
const unifiedCmd = `localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env)"
localcloud status

# Add an opt-in service when a test needs it
localcloud start --local-only --services firestore,gcs,pubsub,bigquery`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="single-container-gcp-beyond-three-emulators"
  kind="Perspective"
  deck="Google's own emulators are good at what they cover. They cover a handful of services, one process each. Most applications use more than that, and the gap gets filled with scripts. There is a simpler shape: one container, one command."
  primary={{ href: '/localstack-for-google-cloud/', label: 'LocalStack for Google Cloud' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/services/', label: `Browse all ${availableServiceCount} services` }}
  {contents}
  next={{ posts: ['inside-localcloud-multiplexing-20-services', 'missing-bigquery-emulator-local-sql'], doc: { href: '/compare/emulators/', label: 'Emulator comparisons, service by service' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="handful">A handful of emulators and a shell script</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Developers arriving from AWS expect to start one thing and have a local cloud. On Google Cloud the official route is the <code>gcloud</code> emulators: Pub/Sub, Firestore, Datastore, Bigtable and Spanner. Each is well made and maintained by the team that runs the real service.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Each is also its own download, its own start command, its own port and its own environment variable. And the list stops there. An application that also uses BigQuery, Cloud Storage, Secret Manager or Cloud Tasks has nothing official to run for those.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">So every team writes the same script: start three processes, wait for each, export five variables, and hope the next engineer's machine agrees. The script becomes a small project that nobody owns.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="compare">Separate emulators or one runtime</h2>
    <table class="blog-compare mt-5">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Separate official emulators</th><th scope="col">LocalCloud</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Services</th><td>The five Google publishes</td><td>{availableServiceCount}, including BigQuery and Cloud Storage</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Start</th><td>One command per emulator</td><td><code>localcloud start</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Configure SDKs</th><td>One variable per emulator, set by hand</td><td><code>eval "$(localcloud env)"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Readiness</th><td>Your script polls each process</td><td>One health check for everything</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Services talking to each other</th><td>Not connected</td><td>Connected: a bucket upload can publish to a topic</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Looking at the data</th><td>Command line only</td><td>A web console with a data browser and SQL editor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-4">If your application uses one service and Google publishes its emulator, that emulator is a fine choice. The case for one runtime starts when a workflow crosses services, because that is where separate processes can't follow.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="opt-in">Heavy services wait until you ask</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">One container doesn't mean everything is always running. The default set starts in about five seconds. Services that need more memory or their own machinery, such as Firestore, Cloud Run and GKE, stay off until you name them with <code>--services</code> or in <code>localcloud.yaml</code>.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">That keeps the laptop cost proportional to what the test in front of you uses, and it means adding a service to a project is a one-word change instead of a new section in the script.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Start it</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Install the CLI from the <a href="/docs/#install-the-cli">quick start</a>, then:</p>
    <BlogCode label="One runtime, one environment" code={unifiedCmd} copy="copy-blog-single-container-cmd" />
    <p class="mt-4"><code>localcloud status</code> lists what is running and where. The <a href="/compare/emulators/">emulator comparisons</a> go service by service if you want to see how a specific one lines up with Google's. Two things stay in the cloud: Compute Engine virtual machines and Vertex AI models.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
